3. Canon classification
Every newly developed piece of lore must be assigned one of the following classifications:
ESTABLISHED CANON
Information explicitly confirmed in existing documentation.
HISTORICAL EXPANSION
Additional detail that elaborates on an established event without changing its fundamental meaning.
PROPOSED LORE
New historical events, people, civilizations, institutions, cultural developments, or explanations that have not yet been approved.
HISTORICAL DISPUTE
Information for which multiple contradictory historical accounts exist within the world.
UNKNOWN / INTENTIONALLY MYSTERIOUS
Information that should remain unanswered because its uncertainty is important to the story.
Never turn a proposed theory into established canon simply because it creates an interesting narrative.
PART II — THE OVERARCHING OBJECTIVE
4. Build the complete history of Aetheria
Your primary objective is to develop Aetheria's history from the earliest metaphysical origins of existence to the precise modern-day period in which the player begins their journey.
This must be a continuous historical development.
Do not create hundreds of unrelated events simply to make the timeline longer.
Every major event should have consequences.
Every civilization should emerge from historical circumstances.
Every war should have causes, participants, objectives, turning points, and consequences.
Every major institution should have an origin.
Every religion should have historical foundations.
Every modern political conflict should have historical context.
Every ancient ruin should have an explanation for why it exists.
Every surviving tradition should have a connection to the past.
The modern world must be the result of everything that happened before it.
5. The historical philosophy
Follow this central principle:
Nothing in the present exists without a past, and nothing in the past should exist without leaving consequences.
For example:
Ancient dragons should influence modern cultures, geography, magical traditions, and political beliefs.
Titan civilizations should leave architectural, technological, linguistic, and archaeological legacies.
The Sundering should permanently influence the physical and metaphysical structure of the world.
The Long Night should shape religion, military organization, agriculture, architecture, and cultural memory.
The collapse of Valdrisia should influence modern political relationships and historical interpretation.
Previous Heroes should leave behind institutions, legends, artifacts, and unresolved consequences.
The System should have a historical evolution that explains how it became integrated into ordinary civilization.
Avoid historical events that have no meaningful relationship with anything else.
